# SFT execution status v0.84

2026-09-13. Three local implementation components are complete and tested:
the full-size coordinator core, read-only input mount primitive, and complete
proposed resource-envelope algebra. This advances the existing Gate 5 import
work; it does not add another roadmap gate. Gate 5 is still incomplete.

## Completed code and verification

- Resource envelope v1: 34 tests. Accounts for both source audits, DCP payload
  allowance, HF content reads, three package traversals, receipt/manifest and
  terminal controls. The frozen private-write maximum is not raised.
- Input mount v1: 37 tests. Checks exact native metadata identities, closed
  inventories, worker ownership, fresh private namespace, read-only input
  views and separate writable output. No source chmod/chown or content read.
- Coordinator v3: 16 tests. Preserves the frozen kernel/package/audit sequence,
  exclusive attempt markers, no retry, prepaid budgets and late-failure rules.
  Its two fixed runtime source modules are hashed before any payload work;
  those additional reads are explicitly prepaid and counted once.

All 87 tests pass on Python 3.11.15 and 3.13.7. Final runs denied network,
subprocess/native calls and ML imports during testing. Inputs were generated
fixtures; kernel and mount operations were test doubles. Six new Python files
passed syntax and 100-column checks. No new native Linux result or Ruff result
is claimed.

Review found and fixed two compatibility defects before handoff: runtime source
files valid at 0644 must not be confused with the data-only 0600 requirement;
and a known runtime source cannot exceed the HF control-read limit. Both fixes
have regression tests. The old importer, host contract, evidence and CI remain
unchanged. New code/evidence are local only; no commit or push was made.

## Scope and remaining integration

The new private coordinator can perform real import I/O when eventually invoked
by an authenticated guardian. It was exercised here only with generated files
and a kernel double. Its public `run_import` still rejects `host_guard_not_bound`.
An underscore/private function is not a security boundary or permission grant.

The mount primitive supplies input/output views, not a complete operating-system
sandbox. The next existing task is to compose the authenticated rootfs and fixed
guardian/bootstrap entry with the new components: preserve read-only software
and input mounts through the final pivot, close authority descriptors, enforce
network/cgroup/resource/privilege controls, and verify the composed successor on
generated Linux fixtures. A later nonrecursive root rebind would discard the
new submounts and must not be used as the integration method.

Dedicated-host/runtime binding, input backing-store and external-writer exclusion,
explicit approved full-size limits and real-host readback remain required before
real acquisition/import. DCP serializer overhead and full-size control output
sufficiency are not proven by synthetic tests. The eventual real import must
still precede the documented GPU-worker/private-data/CUDA-smoke prerequisites
and SFT. No step is being marked complete on component tests alone.

No model/tokenizer/dataset payload, network/S3/instance, GPU, inference or training
operation occurred. No GPU is needed for the next local integration-code work.

